title: "EGMⁿ: The Sequential Endogenous Grid Method"
abstract: |
  Heterogeneous agent models with multiple decisions (consumption, labor, portfolio allocation) face a computational dilemma. Traditional grid search methods evaluate the value function at every possible combination of choices, making them prohibitively expensive as the number of decisions grows. Carroll's (2006) Endogenous Grid Method (EGM) elegantly sidesteps this curse of dimensionality for single-decision problems by using first-order conditions to solve for optimal policy directly, but the method has proven difficult to extend to problems with multiple interacting decisions. This paper resolves that difficulty. I show that when a dynamic optimization problem can be decomposed into a sequence of stages—where each stage represents a distinct decision and its associated state transitions—we can apply EGM sequentially to each stage, preserving its computational advantages while handling multiple decisions. The key insight is that this sequential approach generates policy functions on non-rectilinear grids, which creates an interpolation challenge that standard methods cannot handle efficiently. Gaussian Process Regression provides the solution: it delivers fast, accurate interpolation on these irregular grids while naturally quantifying uncertainty in regions of sparse data. The resulting method—which we call the Sequential Endogenous Grid Method (EGM^n)—solves heterogeneous agent models with multiple decisions orders of magnitude faster than conventional approaches, making previously intractable problems computationally feasible.
